{"causes": ["The sage Vyasa's compilation of the Mahabharata created the original text that required transmission.", "King Janamejaya's desire to hear the full history of his ancestors, the Pandavas and Kauravas, necessitated a comprehensive recitation.", "The ancient Indian tradition of oral storytelling and guru-disciple succession provided the established mechanism for knowledge transfer."], "effects": ["Vaisampayana's recitation established the primary narrative structure through which the Mahabharata was transmitted for centuries.", "His performance solidified the Mahabharata's status as a foundational epic poem in Indian culture and literature.", "The nested narrative structure, initiated by his telling, allowed for the epic's continuous reinterpretation and adaptation across diverse audiences and eras."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "vyasa", "sourceName": "Vyasa", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "vaisampayana", "targetName": "Vaisampayana", "context": "Vyasa directly instructed Vaisampayana in the complete narrative of the Mahabharata, making him a primary disciple."}, {"sourceSlug": "vaisampayana", "sourceName": "Vaisampayana", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "mahabharata", "targetName": "Mahabharata", "context": "Vaisampayana orally transmitted the entire Mahabharata to King Janamejaya, establishing its public recitation tradition."}, {"sourceSlug": "vaisampayana", "sourceName": "Vaisampayana", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "kurukshetra-war", "targetName": "Kurukshetra War", "context": "Vaisampayana's narrative extensively details the events and aftermath of the Kurukshetra War, which forms the core of the Mahabharata."}, {"sourceSlug": "king-janamejaya", "sourceName": "King Janamejaya", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "vaisampayana", "targetName": "Vaisampayana", "context": "King Janamejaya commissioned Vaisampayana to recite the Mahabharata during his Sarpa Satra, creating the context for the epic's public narration."}, {"sourceSlug": "vaisampayana", "sourceName": "Vaisampayana", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "sauti-ugrasrava", "targetName": "Sauti (Ugrasrava)", "context": "Sauti (Ugrasrava) heard Vaisampayana's recitation of the Mahabharata and subsequently retold it to other sages, continuing the epic's transmission chain."}], "places": [{"name": "Hastinapura, India", "role": "Setting for Janamejaya's sacrifice"}, {"name": "Naimisha Forest, India", "role": "Site of Sauti's recitation of Vaisampayana's narrative"}, {"name": "Ancient India", "role": "Geographic scope of epic's influence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Vaisampayana", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "vaisampayana"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2477603", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaisampayana"], "tags": ["Poets & Playwrights", "textual-transmission", "cultural-diffusion", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Vaisampayana's traditional role as the primary narrator of the Mahabharata solidified the epic's narrative structure and ensured its wide dissemination, profoundly shaping Indian cultural, religious, and literary traditions for millennia. His transmission was crucial for the enduring legacy of one of the world's longest epic poems.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-09T06:25:28Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42572657903", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Vaishampayana is the traditional narrator of the Mahabharata, one of the two major Sanskrit epics of India. He was one of Vyasa's four main disciples. His nephew and disciple Yajnavalkya was also a well-known sage.", "newValue": "Vaisampayana, a central figure in the traditional transmission of the Mahabharata, served as the primary narrator of the epic to King Janamejaya during a grand snake sacrifice (Sarpa Satra). As one of the four principal disciples of Vyasa, the legendary compiler of the Vedas and the Mahabharata, Vaisampayana's recitation forms the foundational narrative layer of the epic as it has been known for millennia, solidifying his role as a crucial conduit for ancient Indian wisdom and storytelling. His traditional role underscores the paramount importance of guru-disciple lineages in classical Indian intellectual and spiritual traditions.

According to the *Adi Parva*, the first book of the Mahabharata, Vaisampayana recounted the entire epic, comprising approximately 100,000 verses, over several days at the request of King Janamejaya, who sought to understand the lineage and deeds of his ancestors, the Pandavas and Kauravas. This monumental oral performance, which took place at the snake sacrifice organized by Janamejaya to avenge his father Parikshit's death by a snake, effectively transformed Vyasa's original composition into a public narrative. Vaisampayana's detailed narration, learned directly from Vyasa, ensured the preservation and dissemination of the epic's vast historical, philosophical, and ethical content across ancient India.

The narrative framework established by Vaisampayana's recitation became the standard for subsequent retellings and interpretations of the Mahabharata. His version, as heard by the sage Sauti (Ugrasrava), who then recited it to other sages in the Naimisha Forest, created a nested narrative structure that allowed for the epic's vast scope and complex themes to be conveyed and reinterpreted across generations. This transmission chain highlights the oral tradition's paramount role in preserving and evolving classical Indian literature, with Vaisampayana as its pivotal initial link."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-09T06:25:28Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42572657903", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Poetic traditions and dramatic performance arts", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The sage Vyasa's compilation of the Mahabharata created the original text that required transmission.", "King Janamejaya's desire to hear the full history of his ancestors, the Pandavas and Kauravas, necessitated a comprehensive recitation.", "The ancient Indian tradition of oral storytelling and guru-disciple succession provided the established mechanism for knowledge transfer."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-09T06:25:28Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42572657903", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed poetry and drama to cultural canon", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "Classical"}], "newValue": ["Vaisampayana's recitation established the primary narrative structure through which the Mahabharata was transmitted for centuries.", "His performance solidified the Mahabharata's status as a foundational epic poem in Indian culture and liter